### What is this question really testing?

The interviewer is checking that you manage risk during large technical change. A big-bang cutover that works is luck; an incremental migration with verification at each step is engineering.

```java
package com.bank.interview;
public class MigrationRisk {
    enum Approach { BIG_BANG, INCREMENTAL }
    public static String assess(Approach approach) {
        return switch (approach) {
            case BIG_BANG     -> "High risk: if it fails, everything breaks";
            case INCREMENTAL  -> "Low risk: fallback always ready, parity verified";
        };
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(assess(Approach.INCREMENTAL));
    }
}
```

### What is the strangler-fig pattern?

The new system gradually takes over pieces of the old one while both run side by side. You route a configurable percentage of traffic to the new service.

```java
package com.bank.service;
import java.util.Random;
public class StranglerRouter {
    private static final int NEW_SERVICE_PERCENT = 20;
    private final Random random = new Random();
    public String route(String transferId) {
        if (random.nextInt(100) < NEW_SERVICE_PERCENT) {
            return "NEW_SERVICE:" + transferId;
        }
        return "OLD_MONOLITH:" + transferId;
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        StranglerRouter router = new StranglerRouter();
        for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
            System.out.println(router.route("TXN-" + (5000 + i)));
        }
    }
}
```

### How do you verify parity between old and new?

Run each request through both old and new logic, compare the results, and serve the trusted old result if they disagree. This protects customers while proving the new system.

```java
package com.bank.service;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.logging.Logger;
public class ParityChecker {
    private static final Logger log = Logger.getLogger(ParityChecker.class.getName());
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        BigDecimal oldResult = new BigDecimal("50000.00");
        BigDecimal newResult = new BigDecimal("50000.00");
        boolean match = oldResult.compareTo(newResult) == 0;
        if (match) {
            log.info("Parity PASS: old=" + oldResult + " new=" + newResult);
        } else {
            log.warning("Parity FAIL: serving old result, blocking rollout increase");
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Parity PASS: old=50000.00 new=50000.00
```

### How do you shift traffic gradually?

Start at a small percentage, hold until mismatches stay at zero, then increase: twenty percent, fifty percent, one hundred percent.

```java
package com.bank.service;
public class TrafficShift {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] phases = {5, 20, 50, 100};
        for (int pct : phases) {
            System.out.println("Phase: " + pct + "% to new service");
            System.out.println("  Gate: zero mismatches for 48 hours before next phase");
        }
    }
}
```

### What are the real challenges in a banking migration?

Data consistency between old and new, behavioral parity (same fees, same rounding), and team coordination across multiple services.

```java
package com.bank.interview;
import java.util.List;
public class MigrationChallenges {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> challenges = List.of(
            "Data consistency: old and new must agree on balances",
            "Behavioral parity: same fees, same rounding, same edge cases",
            "Team coordination: 3 teams must align on contract changes",
            "Rollback safety: can revert at any phase without data loss"
        );
        challenges.forEach(c -> System.out.println("  - " + c));
    }
}
```

### How do you quantify the outcome?

Measure what improved: deployment time, failure rate, or latency. "Deployment time dropped from a slow monolith release to minutes" is proof.

```java
package com.bank.service;
import java.util.logging.Logger;
public class MigrationOutcome {
    private static final Logger log = Logger.getLogger(MigrationOutcome.class.getName());
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        log.info("Deployment time: 2 hours (monolith) -> 8 minutes (service)");
        log.info("Customer incidents during migration: 0");
        log.info("Behavioral mismatches: 3 found and fixed during canary, 0 in production");
    }
}
```

### What is the key discipline?

Verify parity continuously. Do not trust the new system on faith. The data must prove it behaves exactly like the old one before you rely on it.

```java
package com.bank.interview;
public class MigrationDiscipline {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Trust is earned by data, not assumed by optimism.");
        System.out.println("Only increase rollout when parity is proven.");
        System.out.println("Always keep the fallback ready.");
    }
}
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

Expect: "What would you do differently?" (start canary earlier), "How long did it take?" (give a specific timeline), and "What if parity failed?" (serve old result, fix mismatch, re-verify).

```java
package com.bank.interview;
public class MigrationFollowUps {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Differently? -> Start canary earlier to find mismatches sooner");
        System.out.println("How long? -> 6 weeks from first canary to 100% rollout");
        System.out.println("Parity failed? -> Serve old result, fix, re-verify, then continue");
    }
}
```
